Indeed.  When I looked at the code I was tempted to declare
the bug impossible and the report spurious, but experience
has taught me not to be too hasty about such things.

The thing is, the player does not (and can not possibly)
gain any nutrition from attacking a cockatrice in this
manner, so breaking foodless conduct seems odd from that
perspective.  On the other hand, now that you mention the
death descrption being "swallowing a cockatrice whole",
that *does* seem to suggest consumption, after a fashion.

I can see several possible ways to resolve this...

 1. Declare it not to be a bug and move on.
 2. Change the death message to avoid giving the impression
    that the cockatrice was actually ingested.
 3. Change the attack message to indicate that you *did*
    ingest it (easy, since it's aleady an isolated case in
    the code), increase the player's nutrition (which would
    only matter if the player is lifesaved), and violate
    the foodless conduct.
 4. Change the death message, and also change the attack
    message, so that they both clearly indicate the player
    was petrified merely by touching the cockatrice, before
    having a chance to ingest it.
 5. Do 4, plus change it from an instastone to normal, slow
    stoning:  "As you come into contact with your intended
    meal, you feel yourself beginning to slow down.  Your
    would-be prey escapes."  This has the advantage of
    leaving the c, so that if you get lifesaved you still
    have to deal with it.  On the other hand, you could
    now use the usual limber-me-up methods (e.g., lizard %)
    rather than blowing through an amulet of life saving
    (or dying if you don't have one).

What does anyone else think?   *Should* being petrified by
attacking a cockatrice (as a purple w or a t) violate the
foodless conduct?

My own instinct would be to go with 1 or maybe 2, on the
grounds that the simplest solutions are often the least
disruptive to game balance.  But maybe that's just me?


